{"comments":[{"diff":"for (vm_mapping_range, vm_mapping_perms) in protect_mappings {
    ...
    let vm_mapping = inner.remove(&vm_mapping_range.start).unwrap();
    ...
    inner.insert_try_merge(taken);","file":"kernel/src/vm/vmar/vmar_impls/protect.rs","fix":"Do not keep stale mapping starts across calls to `inner.insert_try_merge()`. Remove or drain all affected `VmMapping`s before reinserting any protected ranges, or defer merging until after the loop has processed every pre-collected range. This ensures a later `protect_mappings` entry cannot be removed before its turn.","grounding":"Kernel panic reachable from user input","line":46,"persona":"security","problem":"An unprivileged process can build a user-controlled `VMAR` layout that makes this `unwrap()` panic. For example, map three writable anonymous pages, call `mprotect()` on the middle page to make the layout `RW | R | RW`, then call `mprotect()` over all three pages with `PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE`. When the middle mapping is reinserted, `inner.insert_try_merge(taken)` can merge it with the already-writable next mapping and remove that next mapping from `inner.vm_mappings`. The next loop iteration still uses the stale `vm_mapping_range.start`, so `inner.remove(...).unwrap()` can panic in the kernel from user input.","severity":"critical"}]}

